{"schema":"clawhub.skill.verify.v1","ok":true,"decision":"pass","reasons":[],"slug":"google-meet-meetings","displayName":"Google Meet","pageUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/hith3sh/skills/google-meet-meetings","publisherHandle":"hith3sh","publisherDisplayName":"Jay","publisherProfileUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/hith3sh","version":"1.0.6","resolvedFrom":"version","tag":null,"createdAt":1780989018740,"card":{"available":true,"path":"skill-card.md","url":"https://wry-manatee-359.convex.site/api/v1/skills/google-meet-meetings/card?ownerHandle=hith3sh&version=1.0.6","sha256":"74859e8f60adc8e4eaa4281360a36639ee84d53e55e0ed9f14c05404481d7b14","size":2728,"contentType":"text/markdown; charset=utf-8"},"artifact":{"sourceFingerprint":"33006d521dc47c0c8a3cacc31d6a55e57041dbd2c73e91993e8be5020124967e","bundleFingerprints":["2abaae5e5cc6ef4df67ad524d956f7a6d891bd14fdeaec55632a2fa6047639ed","edb1f7db124d85cae65cc2a14d39a32c7adf3a1c227bb3cbd610cd668a955418","2fcccecabc18c9a185ada3240702dccb7c9829c4ea1cbc4ea413872a4f45215c"],"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":14051,"sha256":"0d0b78b071c0f2d04651f7f07767ca5f6bb363f98b51f8dbac925f5b1916fda0","contentType":"text/markdown"}]},"provenance":{"source":"unavailable","reason":"No server-resolved GitHub import provenance is stored for this version."},"security":{"status":"clean","passed":true,"rawStatus":"clean","verdict":"benign","confidence":"high","summary":"This appears to be a legitimate Google Meet integration, but installing it means trusting a third-party ClawLink plugin with access to Meet data.","model":null,"checkedAt":1789545142777,"scannerReports":{"aig":{"version":"2.1.0","$schema":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json","runs":[{"tool":{"driver":{"name":"aig-skill-scan","version":"0.2.1","informationUri":"https://github.com/Tencent/AI-Infra-Guard","rules":[{"id":"T01","name":"Skill Instruction Hijacking","shortDescription":{"text":"Alters the agent's current session goals or safety constraints when the skill is loaded"}},{"id":"T02","name":"Agent Memory Poisoning","shortDescription":{"text":"Writes attacker-controlled rules into persistent memory so they continue to affect future sessions"}},{"id":"T03","name":"Remote Payload Retrieval and Execution","shortDescription":{"text":"Fetches code from an external URL, allowing the effective payload to change after skill review"}},{"id":"T04","name":"Embedded Malicious Code","shortDescription":{"text":"Ships malicious scripts inside the skill package and executes them locally when invoked"}},{"id":"T05","name":"Unauthorized Access and Privilege Escalation","shortDescription":{"text":"Breaks least-privilege boundaries by obtaining permissions beyond the task's legitimate needs"}},{"id":"T06","name":"System Persistence","shortDescription":{"text":"Installs cross-session backdoors, hooks, services, or scheduled tasks that survive the skill run"}},{"id":"T07","name":"Tool Hijacking and Spoofing","shortDescription":{"text":"Modifies, wraps, spoofs, or replaces tools so legitimate-looking calls execute attacker logic"}},{"id":"T08","name":"Insecure Dependencies","shortDescription":{"text":"Introduces malicious packages or components through dependency confusion, typosquatting, or unsafe sources"}},{"id":"T09","name":"Insecure Skill Coding Practices","shortDescription":{"text":"Exposes exploitable flaws such as hardcoded secrets, command injection, plaintext sensitive data, or unsafe temp files"}},{"id":"other","name":"Other Risk","shortDescription":{"text":"Security issues not covered by the T01-T09 classification"}}]}},"results":[{"ruleId":"T08","level":"warning","message":{"text":"Unpinned Third-Party Plugin Installation and Global Enablement"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"SKILL.md"},"region":{"startLine":43,"endLine":50}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"6d360acf094c07a8"},"properties":{"description":"## Vulnerability Details\n\n**File Location**: `SKILL.md`, lines 43–50  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Unpinned third-party dependency installation and enablement  \n**Risk Level**: Medium\n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n```markdown\n## Install\n\nInstall the verified ClawLink plugin from ClawHub. After the user confirms, complete the full setup so ClawLink tools are visible in the next OpenClaw chat.\n\n```bash\nopenclaw plugins install clawhub:clawlink-plugin\nopenclaw config set tools.alsoAllow '[\"clawlink-plugin\"]' --strict-json\nopenclaw gateway restart\n```\n```\n\nEquivalent enablement and restart commands are repeated in the troubleshooting procedure at `SKILL.md`, lines 273–280.\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nThe skill instructs the agent to install `clawlink-plugin` without specifying an immutable version, cryptographic digest, or signature-verification procedure. It then adds the plugin to `tools.alsoAllow` and restarts the OpenClaw gateway so the newly installed component becomes active.\n\nConsequently, the code ultimately executed depends on whichever plugin release the external package source serves at installation time. Its behavior may change after this skill has been reviewed. The audited project contains only `SKILL.md`; it does not include the plugin implementation, so the plugin's network activity, credential handling, permission boundaries, and enforcement of user confirmation cannot be verified from the artifact.\n\nThe documentation also states that ClawLink stores OAuth tokens, proxies Google Meet requests, and can process participant information, recordings, and transcripts. These activities match the declared integration purpose, but they increase the sensitivity of a supply-chain compromise.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. An attacker compromises the plugin publisher account, distribution service, release process, or another dependency used by a later plugin release.\n2. A user or agent follows the skill's unpinned installation command.\n3. The external source supplies the compromised release under the expected package name.\n4. The skill adds that plugin to `tools.alsoAllow`.\n5. The OpenClaw gateway restarts and loads the compromised plugin.\n6. When the user pairs an account or invokes Google Meet tools, the plugin can abuse the data and capabilities available to it.\n\nThis is a conditional supply-chain exploitation path. The reviewed artifact does not establish that the current plugin is malicious or compromised.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nSuccessful exploitation could expose data processed through the integration, potentially including OAuth-mediated requests, meeting-space metadata, conference records, participant information, recordings, and transcripts. Depending on the permissions and isolation applied by OpenClaw, a compromised plugin could also issue unauthorized Google Meet operations or misuse its enabled tool access.\n\nThe exact host privileges and OAuth scopes cannot be established because neither the plugin implementation nor its permission manifest is included in the audited project.","severity":"Medium"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Pin `clawlink-plugin` to a reviewed, immutable version rather than installing the latest package implicitly.\n2. Verify a publisher signature or documented cryptographic digest before enabling the plugin.\n3. Include the relevant plugin source and permission manifest in the review scope, or provide a reproducible-build reference to the exact audited release.\n4. Require explicit user approval before each security-sensitive setup step: installation, allowlisting, account pairing, and gateway restart.\n5. Restrict the plugin to only the Google Meet tools and OAuth scopes required for the requested operation.\n6. Document the OAuth scopes, token encryption and retention controls, revocation procedure, data-processing boundaries, and incident-response process.\n7. Isolate third-party plugins from unrelated host resources and credentials.\n8. Avoid automatically repeating installation or allowlisting commands during troubleshooting without first verifying the installed package's version, integrity, and publisher."}}]}],"properties":{"securityScore":75,"primaryLanguage":"Markdown","llm":"gpt-5.6","startTime":1789545048.0158434,"endTime":1789545098.2106678}}]},"skillspector":{"skill":{"name":"google-meet-meetings","source":"/tmp/clawhub-codex-scan-v57em2vjy4r4ezbp6r78tgrmrx8eg42z-FsD55c/clawscan-skillspector-197469337/artifact","scanned_at":"2026-09-16T07:50:46.532859+00:00"},"risk_assessment":{"score":0,"severity":"LOW","recommendation":"CAUTION","max_issue_severity":"NONE"},"components":[{"path":"SKILL.md","type":"markdown","lines":306,"executable":false,"size_bytes":14051,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null}],"structured_summaries":[],"issues":[],"suppressed_count":0,"suppressed":[],"metadata":{"has_executable_scripts":false,"skillspector_version":"2.11.2","llm_requested":true,"llm_available":true,"meta_analysis_applied":false,"inference_usage":[{"node":"semantic_developer_intent","request_kind":"structured_output","provider":"openai","model":"gpt-5.4-2026-03-05","model_source":"provider_response","usage_source":"provider_response","prompt_tokens":5319,"completion_tokens":14,"cached_tokens":0,"reasoning_tokens":0,"total_tokens":5333},{"node":"semantic_quality_policy","request_kind":"structured_output","provider":"openai","model":"gpt-5.4-2026-03-05","model_source":"provider_response","usage_source":"provider_response","prompt_tokens":5366,"completion_tokens":14,"cached_tokens":0,"reasoning_tokens":0,"total_tokens":5380},{"node":"semantic_security_discovery","request_kind":"structured_output","provider":"openai","model":"gpt-5.4-2026-03-05","model_source":"provider_response","usage_source":"provider_response","prompt_tokens":4898,"completion_tokens":14,"cached_tokens":0,"reasoning_tokens":0,"total_tokens":4912}],"filtering_mode":"heuristic","llm_calls_attempted":3,"llm_calls_succeeded":3},"execution_successful":true,"analysi